      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs' season didn't just stumble - it imploded, ending with a soul-crushing seven-game losing streak that left them dead last in the Atlantic at 78 points, 31 games behind the playoff line. But from that wreckage, a new identity is rising: one hungry to take <strong>bigger swings in free agency</strong> rather than tinkering with the margins. The Leafs are done playing small; they're ready to reshuffle the roster with cap space that could finally match their ambition. Troy Stecher, thrilled with his new two-year, $2.7M extension, is the first real piece of that promise - a gritty, reliable presence who'll anchor the back end while the front office hunts for its next breakout star. With the Atlantic Division's 8th-place finish serving as a stark reminder of what's not working, the focus now shifts to the draft and the open market, where John Chayka and the front office must prove they can build a contender, not just a playoff hopeful. <strong>What to watch for this summer: whether the Leafs make a splash with a top-tier free agent or lean into their draft picks to rebuild from the inside out.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 1, 2026"
      },
